  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:JcDBrIvLD_8",
    "purpose": "Review of the Levi's Vintage Clothing 1947 501XX raw selvedge jeans, covering fit and sizing, construction details and the creator's theory about why they cost $260.",
    "video_type": "review",
    "thesis": "The LVC 1947 501 is a carefully reproduced vintage jean. Its $260 price is hard to explain by materials alone. The creator theorises that it comes from exclusivity: each LVC year line needs its own period-accurate hardware.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "LVC (Levi's Vintage Clothing) reproduces vintage Levi's designs; the 1947 501 is a reproduction of the post-WWII 501, without suspender buttons or a back cinch.",
      "Levi's recommends sizing up two in the waist and adding length for the shrink-to-fit unsanforized denim; the creator bought too small.",
      "The different LVC 501 years (1954, 1955, 1966, etc.) all fit differently.",
      "The denim is 12 oz unsanforized selvedge from a Japanese mill, about 14 oz after shrinking, and it develops leg twist.",
      "Details include iron buttons, copper rivets, hidden rivets in the back pockets and a cowhide patch; whether the thread is cotton or polyester is unclear.",
      "LVC denim now comes from Japan and the jeans are sewn in Turkey, where they used to be US-made.",
      "Price comparisons are made with Sugarcane's 1947 reproduction (~$200) and LSG Denim (~$198).",
      "The creator's price theory, drawing on a Naked & Famous comment, is that exclusivity drives the price, because each LVC year line uses different hardware."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Intro and what LVC is",
        "summary": "Michael introduces the $260 LVC 1947 jeans, admits his pair is too small, and explains LVC as Levi's vintage reproduction line. He also tells how a Levi's fire lost the original records, so the jeans were recreated from existing pairs.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Overview: the 1947 501",
        "summary": "Historical changes in the post-war 501: no suspender buttons, no back cinch, and wartime painted arcs on the back pockets. Then the video's three-part layout is explained.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Fit and sizing",
        "summary": "Levi's sizing advice for shrink-to-fit 501s (size up in waist and length), the tub soak, and a note that each LVC year model has a different fit.",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Denim details",
        "summary": "Covers the source of the denim: originally Cone Mills, now a Japanese mill reproducing the 1947 fabric. Also its weight before and after shrinking, leg twist, hair, slub and nep, and American versus Japanese denim character.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "Hardware, thread and hidden rivets",
        "summary": "Iron buttons and rivets, uncertainty about cotton versus polyester thread, and the hidden back-pocket rivets along with the wear around them.",
        "first_segment": 8,
        "last_segment": 9
      },
      {
        "title": "Why these jeans cost so much",
        "summary": "Uses a Naked & Famous comment on a Stridewise video to argue that exclusivity drives price. The section covers the move from US production to Japanese denim and Turkish manufacturing, and compares prices with Sugarcane and LSG Denim. It ends on the theory that each LVC year line needs unique hardware.",
        "first_segment": 9,
        "last_segment": 14
      }
    ],
